<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1944 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ey Iran</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fa" dir="rtl">ای ایران</bdi> / <bdi lang="fa-Latn">Ey Irân</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A patriotic song written in Iran in 1944, during the Second World War, that many Iranians still sing about their country.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Hossein Gol-e-Golab</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Ruhollah Khaleghi</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Iranians; later the Iranian diaspora</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tehran, Iran</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 27 October 1944</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Hossein Gol-e-Golab and music by Ruhollah Khaleghi (first performed 1944))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ey-iran">Ey Iran</a>&quot; was made during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In September 1941 Allied forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> after the Anglo-Soviet invasion that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>. The poet Hossein Gol-e-Golab was moved to write a poem on Iranian nationalism when he saw Allied flags flying over an Iranian military barracks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>; by his own account, a confrontation between a British soldier and an Iranian officer encouraged him further. He later recalled that the presence of the occupying armies in the streets in 1944 inspired the piece, and that it reached the people despite political opposition. Ruhollah Khaleghi set the poem to music, and Gholam-Hossein Banan sang it.</p><p>It was first performed over two consecutive nights from 27 October 1944 at the military primary school on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, with Banan as soloist. The audience called for several encores. The Minister of Culture then commissioned a studio recording, which Radio Tehran broadcast daily.</p><p>In the transitional period immediately after the 1979 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a>, the song briefly served as the country's de facto national anthem under the interim government. After the Islamic takeover in 1979, several people involved in its making were imprisoned, and public performance of the song was discouraged. Its melody nevertheless continued to circulate and served as a morale-boosting symbol during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran-iraq-war">Iran–Iraq War</a>.</p><p>In 1990 Golnoush Khaleghi, the composer's daughter, arranged a new version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> for the 25th anniversary of her father's death; it appeared on the album &quot;May Nab&quot;, issued by Sorush Publications, with a monologue by Rashid Vatandoust. Since 1979 the song has been widely sung at marches and demonstrations of the Iranian diaspora against the Islamic Republic, and as a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s pre-1979 identity.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic song about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, written while the country was under foreign occupation. Its mood is proud and solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="3772904" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="3772910" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Banan-ey-iran-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-ruhollah-khaleghi-ey-iran-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
